Harry, played by Jeff Daniels.

9
00:40:09,230 --> 00:40:15,230
Stephens, played by Alan Ruck.

10
00:44:36,923 --> 00:44:42,923
Speed is a 1994 American action thriller film directed by Jan de Bont in
his feature directorial debut, and written by Graham Yost.

11
00:49:04,615 --> 00:49:10,615
Keanu Reeves, Dennis Hopper, and Sandra Bullock star in the film,
alongside Joe Morton and Jeff Daniels in supporting roles.

12
00:53:32,307 --> 00:53:38,307
The plot centers on a city bus rigged by a vengeful extortionist Howard Payne
(Hopper) to explode if its speed drops below 50 miles per hour (80 km/h).

13
00:58:00,000 --> 00:58:06,000
Reeves plays LAPD officer Jack Traven, who is tasked with preventing the disaster, with
Bullock portraying a passenger who becomes unexpectedly involved in the mission.

14
01:02:27,692 --> 01:02:33,692
The film was inspired by both Runaway Train and the 1975 Japanese
language action thriller film The Bullet Train.

15
01:06:55,384 --> 01:07:01,384
Screenwriter Graham Yost was told by his father, Canadian television host Elwy Yost, about a 1985 film
called Runaway Train starring Jon Voight, about a train that speeds out of control.

16
01:11:23,076 --> 01:11:29,076
Elwy mistakenly believed that the train's situation was due to a bomb on
board: such a theme had in fact been used in The Bullet Train.

17
01:15:50,769 --> 01:15:56,769
After seeing the Voight film, Graham decided that it would have been better if there had been a bomb on
board a bus with the bus being forced to travel at 20 mph to prevent an actual explosion.

18
01:20:18,461 --> 01:20:24,461
A friend suggested that this be increased to 50 mph.

19
01:24:46,153 --> 01:24:52,153
Speed premiered on June 7, 1994, and was released on
June 10, 1994, by 20th Century Fox.

20
01:29:13,846 --> 01:29:19,846
The film was a sleeper hit, receiving positive reviews from critics and grossing $350 million
worldwide against a $30–37 million budget, becoming the fifth-highest-grossing film of 1994.

21
01:33:41,538 --> 01:33:47,538
At the 67th Academy Awards, Speed won Best Sound.

22
01:38:09,230 --> 01:38:15,230
The film also won Best Sound and Best Editing at the
48th British Academy Film Awards.

23
01:42:36,923 --> 01:42:42,923
A sequel, Speed 2: Cruise Control, was released on June 13, 1997, but performed poorly and was critically
lambasted, receiving widespread criticism for Reeves's absence and the film's change in setting.

24
01:47:04,615 --> 01:47:10,615
The film explores themes of s.w.a.t., bomber, bus bomb,
los angeles, california, falling elevator.

25
01:51:32,307 --> 01:51:38,307
The tone is intense.
